# Deploy Athena

Deploy Athena is a Node.js web app (no framework, no build step) that lets an
operator deploy/update the "Athena" drowsiness-detection software on a fleet
of remote Linux machines ("HUBs") over SSH, from a single browser dashboard.

The operator opens the dashboard, selects HUBs from a table (populated from
`hubs.csv`), and triggers an action (deploy, update binary, PERCLOS on/off,
vibration on/off). The server runs the SSH commands against each selected HUB
in the background and pushes progress back to the browser in real time.

## Entry point

```bash
npm install
npm run lts     # node src/ws-server.js  →  http://localhost:8080
```

`src/ws-server.js` is the only entry point in this checkout — it serves the
dashboard (`src/views/ws-page.html`) over plain HTTP and pushes state/log
updates over a WebSocket connection on the same port (`8080`).

## How the WebSocket layer is built

Node has no built-in WebSocket server, and this project intentionally avoids
adding a dependency (`ws`, `socket.io`, etc.) for it. `src/ws.js` is a small,
hand-rolled implementation of the server side of the WebSocket protocol
([RFC 6455](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6455)): it does the HTTP
Upgrade handshake, and parses/builds the raw frames itself (opcode, payload
length incl. the 126/127 extended-length cases, and unmasking client
frames).

This was written following an existing "from scratch" walkthrough found
online, credited in `md/lts.md`:

> Implementing a WebSocket Server from Scratch in Node.js — Better Programming (Medium)
> https://medium.com/better-programming/implementing-a-websocket-server-from-scratch-in-node-js-a1360e00a95f

That article is the reference model for `WebSocketServer` in `src/ws.js`
(the handshake using the `Sec-WebSocket-Accept` GUID, and the manual frame
parsing/masking). Read it if you need to touch `_init`, `parseFrame`,
`createFrame`, or `_unmask` — the code mirrors that model closely, so the
article explains *why* the bit-twiddling is shaped the way it is, not just
what it does.

## Request/message flow

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    Browser["Browser dashboard\n(src/views/ws-page.html + ws-page.js)"]

    subgraph Server["src/ws-server.js (port 8080)"]
        WS["src/ws.js\nWebSocketServer\n(hand-rolled RFC6455 handshake + framing)"]
        Router["message switch\n(task: init / deploy / update /\nperclosOn / perclosOff /\nvibrationOn / vibrationOff)"]
        Controllers["src/controllers/*Ws.controller.js"]
        Services["src/services/*.service.js\n(NodeSSH via src/tools/tools.js)"]
    end

    Hubs[("Remote HUBs\n(SSH, over hubs.csv)")]
    State[("state.json")]
    Logs[("logs/&lt;hub&gt;.log")]

    Browser -- "HTTP GET /" --> WS
    Browser <-- "WebSocket JSON messages" --> WS
    WS --> Router --> Controllers --> Services
    Services -- "ssh2 / node-ssh" --> Hubs
    Services --> State
    Services --> Logs
    Controllers -- "reply() pushes progress" --> WS --> Browser
```

Each controller/service pair follows the same shape: look up the selected
HUBs from `hubs.csv`, open an SSH connection per HUB (`Tools.connectSSH`,
with password fallback), run the remote commands, update `state.json` +
append to `logs/<hub>.log`, and push the new state back over the socket via
the `reply` callback so the dashboard updates without polling.

## Project layout

```
src/
  ws-server.js          entry point — wires the WebSocket server to the controllers
  ws.js                 hand-rolled WebSocket server (see "model from internet" above)
  config/
    config.js           SSH credentials/port, remote path, retry limit
    variables.js         paths for hubs.csv / logs / state.json / update payload
  controllers/          per-action orchestration (deploy, update, perclos, vibration)
  services/             per-action SSH/business logic, returns a status string
  tools/tools.js        shared statics: SSH connect, CSV parsing, state persistence, logging
  views/
    ws-page.html/.js/.css  the dashboard UI served to the browser

hubs.csv                HUB registry (name,ip) — must use Unix (LF) line endings
state.json              per-HUB status persisted across runs (safe to hand-edit/reset)
logs/<hub>.log          append-only per-HUB deploy log
athena.zip              deploy payload uploaded to each HUB via SFTP
fcsv143.tar             payload used by the update flow
md/                     background docs (legacy1-3.md, lts.md)
```

## Status values

`PENDING`, `IN_PROGRESS`, `UPLOADED`, `INSTALLED`, `SUCCESS`, `FAILED`,
`OFFLINE` for deploy, plus feature-specific variants such as
`UPDATE_OK`/`UPDATE_FAILED`, `PERCLOS_ON_OK`/`PERCLOS_OFF_OK`,
`VIB_ON_OK`/`VIB_OFF_OK` — all persisted per HUB IP in `state.json`.

